  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front associated with the system that brought showers and\nsome severe thunderstorms yesterday will continue to cross the \narea through this afternoon. This will be a mostly dry front, \nthrough a very isolated shower or rumble of thunder cannot be \nruled out through this afternoon. High pressure builds in with \nmostly clear weather tonight with temperatures falling back into\nthe 40s and 50s. Some patchy fog will likely develop in the \ntypical river valley areas.\n\nHigh pressure will then bring mostly dry and seasonable weather\nthrough the weekend, though a passing upper level shortwave \ncould bring some isolated to scattered showers and a rumble of \nthunder to some areas by Sunday afternoon into Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nNext week will feature the potential for multiple rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms across the area. An upper level ridge\nwill build across the central CONUS by the end of this weekend\nand expand eastward into the mid-Atlantic and Southeast states\nnext week. We look to be located within the northern periphery \nof this ridge. As a result, multiple shortwaves are expected to \ntrack up and over the ridge near or over our area. This will \nopen the potential for at least one if not multiple rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms (MCS's) to track over our area. \nModerate to strong instability in place along with 30+ kt of \nshear could increase the potential for some strong to severe \nthunderstorms at times. However, the overall timing and \nplacement of such features remains uncertain which leads to \nlower confidence on the most favored days. Parts of our area are\noutlooked in a rare slight risk for severe thunderstorms on \nWednesday per the latest outlook from the Storm Prediction \nCenter, but again, the timing and placement could change in \nfuture outlooks. High temperatures most of next week look to be \nin the 70s to lower 80s with lows each night in the 50s and 60s.",
